WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.040
A failed private rescue leaves NASA's Swift telescope on course for an uncontrolled return to Earth. This is fascinating frontiers, episode 175. A private spacecraft could not capture NASA's Swift Space Telescope, leaving the observatory to re-enter naturally. Details on timing and location remain limited while teams assess the orbit. Swift has operated for nearly two decades, delivering gamma ray and x-ray observations of transient events.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:40.780
Engineers continue to monitor the spacecraft's systems until the final descent. The mission's long record of burst detection stands as one of its primary legacies, now on a different note.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:57.660
Japanese researchers examining data from the Subaru Telescope's 8.2-meter mirror, found comet 28-p-slash nuage men had passed through existing frames at just the right angle. The geometry allowed them to measure the comet's surface properties directly.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:09.220
The Subaru images captured reflectance data, normally hidden by dust and gas. The work forms part of a larger effort to understand why some comets and asteroids share similar traits,

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:18.560
and what those similarities imply for solar system formation. Follow-up observations will test whether other naked comets can be characterized the same way.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:31.500
Point a radio dish at nothing for a thousand hours and you still might miss the moment when an 8.2-meter mirror records the surface of a comet only a few kilometers across from hundreds of millions of kilometers away.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:43.600
In the Subaru archive, the nucleus of 28-p-slash nuage men crossed the field at a phase angle that sent sunlight straight back from bare rock instead of scattering through the usual dust shroud.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:54.480
That brief window yielded reflectance measurements normally masked by the coma, showing a surface whose spectral signature resembles certain asteroids more than typical active comets.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:05.040
The geometry lasted only minutes in the data set, yet supplied the exact illumination needed to separate intrinsic rock properties from the surrounding cloud of ice and dust.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:15.280
Such alignments occur when the comets orbit and the telescopes pointing happened to intersect at the right moment, turning routine survey images into unplanned surface studies.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:24.520
The discovery raises the question of whether other dormant comets hide similar asteroid-like exteriors that only become visible under equally rare conditions.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:35.720
We still do not know whether this naked appearance marks a common end state for comets that have shed their outer layers, or whether it identifies a secret population formed closer to the sun.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:46.820
Shifting focus to laboratory work, University of Hawaii at Manoa chemists identified faster routes for large carbon-based molecules to assemble under space conditions.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:54.140
The findings address a long-standing question about how the building blocks of organic chemistry grow in interstellar environments.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:03:00.660
Laboratory simulations showed reaction sequences that operate on shorter timescales than previously modeled.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:08.180
The team traced specific reaction pathways that produce complex aromatics without requiring extreme conditions.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:13.920
Next steps involve matching the laboratory signatures against spectra from star-forming regions.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:23.960
On another front, Stanford researchers built a model showing how close in lava-covered planets can retain atmospheres despite intense stellar radiation.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:30.980
The work questions earlier assumptions about the cosmic shoreline that separates worlds with and without atmospheres.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.260
Observations of these extreme cases provide new tests for atmospheric loss theory.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:41.480
The simulations incorporate surface outgassing balanced against stellar wind stripping.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:47.420
Follow-up spectroscopy will check whether the predicted retention holds across additional targets.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:54.820
Lava-covered exoplanets close to their stars are somehow keeping atmospheres that models once predicted would be stripped away.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:04:03.600
Stanford's new simulations show that surface outgassing and stellar wind interactions can balance each other in ways not previously calculated.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:09.540
The result forces a fresh look at which worlds might retain the gases needed for further chemistry.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:15.580
Observations of these extreme cases now serve as direct tests for atmospheric retention theories.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.740
What surface or magnetic process actually tips the balance on these extreme planets?

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:23.060
Turning to human health in orbit.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:31.400
A new study isolates possible mechanisms that cause astronauts lower eyelids to lose tone after extended time in microgravity.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:36.120
The changes are associated with shifts in vision that some crew members report.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:42.360
Researchers examined how fluid redistribution and tissue response interact during orbital flight.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.560
Data from multiple missions will help refine countermeasures for future long stays.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:54.040
The findings point to specific physiological responses that differ from those seen in short duration flights.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:05:03.780
In other news, Starmus Festival co-founders Sir Brian May and Garek Israelian presented the 2026 medal to filmmaker Sir Ridley Scott.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:09.720
The award recognizes his Phil's role in bringing science and big questions to wide audiences.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:14.640
Scott's latest project, Dog Stars, premiered globally before the ceremony.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:20.300
The medal has been given annually for a decade to honor science communication through creative work.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:25.260
Recipients are chosen for their ability to connect complex ideas with global viewers.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:27.460
Moving to personnel changes.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:34.480
NASA astronaut and physician Johnny Kim is returning to active military service after completing one space flight.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:40.600
His departure reduces the active astronaut corps by one member with both medical and flight experience.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:45.920
Kim's NASA tenure included training and operation support in addition to his mission.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.260
The agency has not yet named a replacement assignment for his former duties.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:55.640
His background as a medical doctor added specialized capability during his time with the corps.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:03.220
Finally, NASA science published a series of stories detailing the Sentinel-6B ocean surface topography mission.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:09.760
The articles cover instrument performance, data products, and the satellites role in tracking sea-level changes.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:15.240
Sentinel-6B continues the long-term record begun by earlier altimetry missions.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.620
Public access to the new materials begins immediately.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:24.160
The documentation highlights how the satellite contributes to climate monitoring efforts worldwide.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.600
Keep an eye on follow-up spectroscopy results for those lava world atmosphere models.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:32.320
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

